Z||000000||RedOktober||11-24-2001||03:53 AM||||For those that can get away with or choose not to run a fuel injected car anymore, here are a few pics of the Edelbrock Carb manifolds with a holley 390cfm carb.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1base_pn_3785.jpg"> P/N 3785<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2base_pn_3789.jpg"> P/N 3789<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/final_390cfm.jpg"> P/N #0-8007<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000001||Monkeyman||11-24-2001||06:10 AM||||Is there a benefit to a carbed motor?  I was always under the assumption that FI was better for power, emissions, gas mileage, reliability, etc.||||reg||1
Z||000002||westtexas||11-24-2001||07:05 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B>Is there a benefit to a carbed motor?  I was always under the assumption that FI was better for power, emissions, gas mileage, reliability, etc.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> The main benefit will be in the Edelbrock manifold which is design to flow at higher RPMs. Another benefit will be the four 'honking' when the secondaries open up. I love the sound effects. Another great benefit will be a much greater choice of cams. Plus, a change of jets is more hands-on for some people. Man, I've just about talked myself into a carb. <P>Red, what else have you done to the engine and at what RPMs are you looking to make your power?<P>Great pictures, your going to have lots of fun winding it up.<P>Robert<P><p>[This message has been edited by westtexas (edited 11-24-2001).]||||reg||1
